    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To solve the equation \\((2^x - 2)(5 \\cdot 2^x + 10) = 3 \\cdot 4^x + 12\\), we start by expressing \\(4^x\\) as \\((2^2)^x = (2^x)^2\\). This allows us to rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(2^x - 2)(5 \\cdot 2^x + 10) = 3 (2^x)^2 + 12\n\\]\n\nNext, we expand the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n(2^x - 2)(5 \\cdot 2^x + 10) = 2^x \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2^x + 2^x \\cdot 10 - 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2^x - 2 \\cdot 10 = 5 \\cdot (2^x)^2 + 10 \\cdot 2^x - 10 \\cdot 2^x - 20 = 5 (2^x)^2 - 20\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n5 (2^x)^2 - 20 = 3 (2^x)^2 + 12\n\\]\n\nWe then move all terms to one side of the equation to set it to zero:\n\n\\[\n5 (2^x)^2 - 20 - 3 (2^x)^2 - 12 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n2 (2^x)^2 - 32 = 0\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n\\[\n(2^x)^2 - 16 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis can be factored as a difference of squares:\n\n\\[\n(2^x - 4)(2^x + 4) = 0\n\\]\n\nSetting each factor to zero gives us two potential solutions:\n\n\\[\n2^x - 4 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad 2^x + 4 = 0\n\\]\n\nFor \\(2^x - 4 = 0\\):\n\n\\[\n2^x = 4 = 2^2 \\implies x = 2\n\\]\n\nFor \\(2^x + 4 = 0\\):\n\n\\[\n2^x = -4\n\\]\n\nSince \\(2^x\\) is always positive for any real number \\(x\\), the equation \\(2^x = -4\\) has no real solutions. Therefore, the only real solution is \\(x = 2\\).\n\nThus, the value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."
